How to override method in C++?
==============================
In C++, you override a method using inheritance + virtual functions.

✔ Basic idea of method overriding
==================================
Method overriding means:
A derived class provides its own version of a base class method.

✔ Step 1: Use virtual in base class
===================================
class Base {
public:
    virtual void show() {
        std::cout << "Base class show()" << std::endl;
    }
};

✔ Step 2: Override in derived class
===================================
class Derived : public Base {
public:
    void show() override {
        std::cout << "Derived class show()" << std::endl;
    }
};

✔ Step 3: Use base pointer (important for runtime polymorphism)
===============================================================
int main() {
    Base* obj = new Derived();
    obj->show();  // calls Derived version

    delete obj;
}

✔ Output:
=========
Derived class show()

✔ Key points
=============
virtual in base class enables runtime polymorphism
override in derived class is optional but recommended (helps catch errors)
Method signature must be exactly the same

❌ What happens WITHOUT virtual?
================================
Base* obj = new Derived();
obj->show();

👉 Calls Base class method, not Derived (no runtime polymorphism)

✔ Summary
=========
Concept	                  Requirement
=======                   ===========
Overriding	              Same function signature
Runtime behavior	        virtual keyword in base
Best practice	            Use override in derived


Example (Working Code)
======================
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

// Base class
class Base {
public:
    // virtual function enables method overriding
    virtual void show() {
        cout << "Base class show() function" << endl;
    }
};

// Derived class
class Derived : public Base {
public:
    // overridden function
    void show() override {
        cout << "Derived class show() function" << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    Base* ptr;          // base class pointer
    Derived obj;        // derived class object

    ptr = &obj;         // base pointer points to derived object

    ptr->show();        // runtime polymorphism (method overriding)

    return 0;
}


